# Line-ending policy.
#
# This repo is developed on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites text files to CRLF in
# the working tree (and back to LF in the object store). That is harmless for sources we compile,
# but it is a real bug for anything under agent/src/main/agent/skills/bundled/, because those files
# ship to users verbatim AND are content-hashed:
#
#   * Vite's `?raw` import inlines their bytes straight into out/main/index.js, so whatever EOL the
#     working tree happens to have at build time is what gets written into ~/.klenny/skills/ on a
#     user's machine.
#   * ~/.klenny/skills-seed-state.json records a sha256 of what was last seeded, and
#     seedBundledSkills()/seedSkillAssets() (agent/src/main/agent/skills/manager.ts) deliberately
#     refuse to overwrite any file whose on-disk hash differs from that record — the assumption
#     being that the user edited it, and a user's edits are never clobbered.
#   * An EOL-only flip is a *byte* difference with *zero* content difference. So without this
#     policy, autocrlf can make a pristine seeded copy look user-edited, which permanently stops
#     that install from receiving bundled-skill updates. It fails silently and looks like nothing
#     at all went wrong.
#   * _legacy/*.md are hashed the same way to decide whether a pre-versioning install is still
#     pristine enough to upgrade (BundledSkill.legacyVariants), so a flipped EOL breaks that
#     migration match too.
#
# Pinning eol=lf keeps every checkout byte-identical, which is what makes those hashes meaningful.
# Verify with: git check-attr text eol -- <path>
agent/src/main/agent/skills/bundled/** text eol=lf
